(Lecture 9 -- Fine-Tuning and LoRA) Tj
T*
T*
(Full fine-tuning updates every weight in a pretrained model. For a 7B) Tj
T*
(parameter model that means 7 billion gradients and 7 billion optimizer) Tj
T*
(states in memory -- Adam alone doubles that -- which is why full) Tj
T*
(fine-tuning of large models is usually out of reach on a single GPU.) Tj
T*
T*
(LoRA \(Low-Rank Adaptation\) freezes the original weight matrix W entirely) Tj
T*
(and learns a much smaller update instead. Rather than learning a full) Tj
T*
(delta-W the same shape as W, LoRA factors the update into two small) Tj
T*
(matrices A and B, of rank r, so that delta-W = B times A. If W is) Tj
T*
(d by k, A is r by k and B is d by r, with r chosen far smaller than) Tj
T*
(d or k -- often 8, 16, or 32. The number of trainable parameters drops) Tj
T*
(from d times k to r times \(d plus k\), which for typical transformer) Tj
T*
(layer sizes is a reduction of two to three orders of magnitude.) Tj
T*
T*
(At inference time the adapter can be merged back into the base weights) Tj
T*
(\(W plus B times A\) with zero added latency, or kept separate so a single) Tj
T*
(base model can swap between many task-specific adapters cheaply -- this) Tj
T*
(is the mechanism that makes serving many fine-tuned variants of one) Tj
T*
(base model practical.) Tj
T*
T*
(Quantization is a complementary technique: it reduces the precision each) Tj
T*
(weight is stored at, typically from 16-bit floats to 8-bit or 4-bit) Tj
T*
(integers, cutting memory further. QLoRA combines both: the frozen base) Tj
T*
(model is quantized to 4 bits, while the small LoRA adapters are trained) Tj
T*
(in higher precision on top of it -- which is what makes it possible to) Tj
T*
(fine-tune a 65B parameter model on a single consumer GPU.) Tj
T*
T*
(The key tradeoff across all of this is rank r. Too low, and the adapter) Tj
T*
(lacks the capacity to represent the target task's shift in behaviour.) Tj
T*
(Too high, and LoRA approaches the cost of full fine-tuning without a) Tj
T*
(clear accuracy benefit -- in practice r is usually chosen well below) Tj
T*
(the point where returns diminish, since low-rank updates generalize) Tj
T*
(better than high-rank ones on small fine-tuning datasets.) Tj
